"Create a compact, publication-quality LaTeX/TikZ schematic on a pure white background, approximately 18 cm wide and 8 cm high. Use a two-row, four-column arrangement with generous spacing, clean mathematical typography, and no figure title. All text should remain legible at manuscript size: approximately 10\u201311 pt for equations and 9\u201310 pt for short descriptive labels. Use rounded rectangular nodes with subtle pastel fills, 0.7 pt outlines, and no shadows, gradients, decorative icons, or three-dimensional effects.\n\nArrange the seven computational elements as follows:\n\n- **Upper left \u2014 supplied inputs.** A pale blue node labeled \u201cInput observations,\u201d containing\n  \\[\n  \\mathbf{x}=(-2,-1,0,1,2).\n  \\]\n- **Lower left \u2014 supplied targets.** A matching pale blue node labeled \u201cObserved targets,\u201d containing\n  \\[\n  \\mathbf{y}=2\\mathbf{x}+3\\mathbf{1}\n  \\]\n  and, on a second line,\n  \\[\n  \\mathbf{y}=(-1,1,3,5,7).\n  \\]\n  Align these two input nodes vertically.\n- **Upper second column \u2014 baseline prediction.** A pale blue-gray node labeled \u201cFixed baseline,\u201d containing\n  \\[\n  \\widehat{\\mathbf{y}}^{(0)}=2\\mathbf{x}\n  \\]\n  followed by\n  \\[\n  (-4,-2,0,2,4).\n  \\]\n  This node should read as a direct arithmetic operation.\n- **Lower second column \u2014 observed residuals.** A pale amber node labeled \u201cObserved residuals,\u201d containing\n  \\[\n  \\mathbf{r}=\\mathbf{y}-2\\mathbf{x}\n  \\]\n  and\n  \\[\n  \\mathbf{r}=(3,3,3,3,3).\n  \\]\n  Position it directly beneath the baseline node and horizontally beside the target node.\n- **Lower third column \u2014 scalar correction.** A pale teal node labeled \u201cMean residual,\u201d containing\n  \\[\n  b=\\frac{1}{5}\\sum_{i=1}^{5}r_i=3.\n  \\]\n  Make this node slightly narrower than the vector-valued nodes, reinforcing that its output is one scalar.\n- **Upper right \u2014 corrected prediction.** A pale teal node labeled \u201cCorrected prediction,\u201d containing\n  \\[\n  \\widehat{\\mathbf{y}}^{\\,\\mathrm{corr}}\n  =2\\mathbf{x}+b\\mathbf{1}\n  \\]\n  and\n  \\[\n  \\widehat{\\mathbf{y}}^{\\,\\mathrm{corr}}\n  =(-1,1,3,5,7).\n  \\]\n- **Lower right \u2014 evaluation.** A very light lavender-gray node labeled \u201cEvaluate on the same five observations.\u201d Beneath the label, display the comparison\n  \\[\n  \\mathbf{y}\\quad\\text{vs.}\\quad\n  \\widehat{\\mathbf{y}}^{\\,\\mathrm{corr}},\n  \\]\n  followed by the compact calculation\n  \\[\n  \\mathrm{MSE}\n  =\\frac{1}{5}\\sum_{i=1}^{5}\n  \\left(y_i-\\widehat y_i^{\\,\\mathrm{corr}}\\right)^2\n  =0.\n  \\]\n  Break the equation across two lines if necessary to preserve readable type.\n\nDraw every dependency explicitly using solid, approximately 0.9 pt arrows with consistent filled arrowheads:\n\n1. A horizontal arrow from the input \\(\\mathbf{x}\\) node to the baseline node.\n2. A downward arrow from the baseline node to the residual node.\n3. A horizontal arrow from the observed-target node to the residual node.\n4. A horizontal arrow from the residual node to the mean-residual node, labeled \u201caverage five residuals.\u201d\n5. A long horizontal arrow from the baseline node to the corrected-prediction node, passing through the open upper third-column space. Label it \\(2\\mathbf{x}\\).\n6. A separate arrow from the mean-residual node to the corrected-prediction node. Route it upward through the open third-column space, then rightward into a lower port on the corrected node\u2019s left edge. Label it \\(b=3\\). Keep this route visibly separate from the baseline arrow.\n7. A downward arrow from the corrected-prediction node to the evaluation node.\n8. A separate target-data arrow from the observed-target node to the evaluation node. Route it downward, horizontally beneath the lower row, then upward into the evaluation node\u2019s bottom edge. Label its horizontal segment \u201cobserved targets: same five points.\u201d\n\nUse dark slate arrows for supplied values and baseline predictions. Emphasize the residual-to-mean and mean-to-correction arrows in a darker teal, making the dependence of \\(b\\) on the observed residuals immediately apparent. Keep all labels dark enough for strong contrast, and avoid arrow crossings or lines passing through nodes.\n\nPlace one small, centered note beneath the diagram: \u201cThe correction is computed and evaluated on these same five observations.\u201d Keep this note visually subordinate to the equations. The figure should communicate the exact finite calculation and its data dependencies, without suggesting held-out evaluation, predictive generalization, optimization, or additional experiments."